crons.message:
⚡ EXEC DISCIPLINE: run any `python3 <path>` command exactly as written — a bare invocation, nothing appended, no shell wrapping, no chaining with other commands, no output redirection, no exit-code capture. Scripts always print one JSON line to stdout with a `status` field (ok/error/degraded); parse that final line to decide what to do. openclaw 2026.4.11 rejects complex shell invocations before they run. Generate the morning news digest. Run: python3 ~/.openclaw/news-digest-workspace/scripts/fetch-and-rank.py. This fetches all RSS feeds and LinkedIn updates, ranks them against the preference model at preferences/model.json, and writes cache/ranked-<date>.json. Read the ranked output. Select the top 15-20 items. For each item, write a one-sentence extended headline explaining why it matters. Also read the ranked file's score fields and group items by topic category (🤖 AI & Tech, 💰 Economics, 🌍 World, 🏛️ US Policy, 🔗 LinkedIn, 📋 Also Noted). 

FORMAT (strict — the engagement loop reads this back via /like N and /dislike N, so item numbers and per-item reaction prompts are load-bearing):

🐛📰 Morning Edition — {long date}

{category-emoji} {Category name}

{N}. {extended headline} — {source}
{url}
👍 /like {N}    👎 /dislike {N}    📖 /more {N}

{N+1}. {extended headline} — {source}
{url}
👍 /like {N+1}    👎 /dislike {N+1}    📖 /more {N+1}

(repeat per item, then blank line before the next category)

End with a single trailing line: 🐛 {total} items · {source_count} sources · Reply inline or with /ask [topic]. If any feed failed, append a short 'Source notes:' line below that.

For LinkedIn message thread items (source=linkedin, source_label contains 'Message'), the title already contains the sender and the summary is an LLM-generated thread summary. Keep the same per-item reaction prompt format — Sam likes/dislikes individual message threads just like news items. 

IMPORTANT: Write the final formatted digest to ~/.openclaw/news-digest-workspace/cache/morning-brief-ready.txt and exit. Do NOT send to Telegram — the fleet-delivery cron at 11:55 UTC (fix-it/morning-fleet-deliver) reads this file, holds until 12:00 UTC exactly, chunks it into multiple messages if >4000 chars, and sends via the NEWSDIGEST_BOT_TOKEN. Update your status file with items count and any errors.
